--- body ---
## Context

Child of `0800`, successor to `0802`.

`0802` shipped `adapters/perch.py`, which makes one named skill discoverable by
Claude Code, Codex and Pi. The skill it manages is a module constant,
`SLICE = "perch"`, used sixteen times and never for anything but a path
component or a message string. Nothing in the module transforms skill content.

That constant was correct for a one-slice pilot: `0802`'s exit criterion only
permits abstractions justified by duplication *observed in the slice*, and one
slice observes none. A second skill is what makes the duplication observable,
and it is one constant wide.

This is explicitly **not** the generator `0800` and `0802` rule out. Taking the
skill name as an argument transforms nothing; `tests/test_perch_pilot.py`'s
`test_the_pilot_introduces_no_generator` stays as it is and must keep passing.
The evidence already says no skills generator will ever be needed: the only
duplication `0802` recorded across three harnesses is `name` + `description`,
both already in the canonical frontmatter. For the skill layer a parameterised
installer is the complete answer, not a step toward a compiler.

## The grammar

Decided with the author on 2026-09-16: **verb, then type, then object**, with
the destination an option rather than a trailing positional, so objects can be
variadic without ambiguity.

    idh install skill perch                  # everywhere it applies
    idh install skill perch roar --to codex  # narrowed, variadic
    idh uninstall skill perch
    idh status skill perch                   # object optional
    idh check harness codex

Three constraints behind that shape, each recorded so a later reader does not
relitigate them:

- **The executable is a noun.** `idh-setup uninstall` is the "click Start to
  shut down" absurdity the harness already rules out; the verb belongs in the
  sub-command.
- **A missing `--to` means every applicable harness.** The tool knows codex and
  pi share `$HOME/.agents/skills` and that claude's root is the canonical source
  itself. Making the operator name each harness produces the "already
  discoverable" no-op that today's `install codex` then `install pi` prints.
- **The type slot is carried from the start.** `0803` ports a script and `0809`
  ports an enforcing guard. A bare object name collides across those types, and
  adding a type later breaks a CLI already written into docs, tickets and
  habits. `idh install script healthcheck` and `idh install guard
  commit-on-main` must fit without a rename.

Only the `skill` type is implemented here. `script` and `guard` are grammar
slots their own tickets fill.

## Relevant files

- `adapters/perch.py` — becomes the skill type's implementation
- `adapters/README.md` — command surface, invocation table
- `adapters/pilot-support.json` — `native_expression` fields quote CLI commands
- `tests/test_perch_pilot.py`, `tests/test_perch_discovery.py` — 65 tests to
  re-point at a named object rather than a constant
- `scripts/shell-init.sh` — where an `idh` entry point reaches PATH

## Actions

1. Replace `SLICE` with an object argument threaded through `canonical_source`,
   `target_path`, `_is_canonical`, `_is_dangling_link`, `status`, `install`,
   `uninstall` and every message that names the slice.
2. Build the verb/type/object parser with nested sub-parsers. `--to` accepts one
   harness; absent, the verb applies to every harness the type supports.
3. Keep `HARNESSES` a closed tuple. The harness axis stays enumerated and
   tested; only the object axis opens.
4. Refuse an unknown object the way an unknown harness is refused today: a
   `Refusal` naming what was looked for and where, never a silent success.
5. Re-point the existing tests at `perch` as a named object, and add one test
   installing a second real skill to prove the object axis is genuinely open.
6. Update `adapters/README.md` and every `native_expression` in
   `pilot-support.json` that quotes a command string.
7. Ship the entry point as `bin/idh`. Invoking it by full path is sufficient
   for this ticket.

Putting `bin/` on PATH is deliberately **not** part of this ticket, though
`scripts/shell-init.sh` adds nothing to PATH today and the four scripts already
in `bin/` are therefore reachable only by full path. That change alters how four
unrelated, already-shipped scripts are invoked -- `snapshot` in particular is a
name likely to shadow or be shadowed -- and it needs its own tests. It belongs
with the lifecycle ticket that absorbs those scripts.

## Out of scope

The IDH lifecycle family -- `setup`, `update`, and absorbing the verb-named
`bin/install-cron`, `bin/snapshot`, `bin/usage-report` and `bin/mammoth-audit`
into noun-first sub-commands -- is a separate ticket. This one ships the
projection family and the grammar that leaves room for the rest.

Relocating the repository out of `~/.claude` is likewise out of scope, and is
owned by the memory programme: `docs/2026-09-10-dragon-memory-design.md` records
the disjoint-roots invariant as known red precisely because IDH's canonical
store sits inside a runtime's native memory root. Nothing here depends on that
move, and a probe on 2026-09-16 confirmed `install claude` already creates a
real symlink when the repository lives elsewhere.

## Test

- Installing two different skills produces two independent links, and
  uninstalling one leaves the other discoverable.
- `idh install skill perch` with no `--to` reports one action per applicable
  harness, and is idempotent on a second run.
- An object with no canonical directory refuses with exit 2 and one stderr
  line, matching the module's existing Refusal contract.
- `test_the_pilot_introduces_no_generator` still passes unchanged.

## Verification

- [ ] `SLICE` is gone; no module-level constant names a specific skill
- [ ] Two skills install, uninstall and report independently, proved by test
- [ ] Absent `--to` covers every applicable harness; the narrowed form still works
- [ ] `HARNESSES` remains a closed, tested tuple
- [ ] README and pilot inventory quote the shipped commands, checked by test
- [ ] `make check` and `./scripts/check-agnostic.sh` pass

## Exit criteria

- [ ] One CLI installs any named skill into any supported harness
- [ ] The grammar leaves `script` and `guard` addable without a breaking rename
- [ ] No generator, IR or content transformation was introduced
